<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Cheng Han</h1><p class="st-meta">304 CE – 347 CE · capital Chengdu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A refugee-founded kingdom in Sichuan, one of the first states established in the collapse of the Western Jin, noted for its light taxes and Daoist influence.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Li Te led Ba and Di refugees from famine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> around 298 and rebelled against Jin officials. After his death his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-xiong">Li Xiong</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> in 304, styling himself King of Chengdu, and in 306 proclaimed himself emperor of Cheng.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-xiong">Li Xiong</a> governed leniently, with simple laws and low taxes, and appointed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Daoist</a> master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fan-changsheng">Fan Changsheng</a> as chancellor, reflecting the strong presence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/way-of-the-five-pecks-of-rice">Celestial Masters</a> tradition among his followers. Later rulers were less able: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-shou">Li Shou</a> seized the throne in 338, renamed the state Han and built extravagant palaces.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 347 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huan-wen">Huan Wen</a> marched up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> and accepted the surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-shi-cheng-han">Li Shi</a>, restoring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> to Chinese dynastic rule.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cheng-han.jpg" alt="Cheng Han" width="320" height="228"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sixteen_Kingdoms_338_AD_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>304 CE – 347 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chengdu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Li Xiong, Li Shou, Li Shi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Daoism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cheng, Later Shu (Sixteen Kingdoms)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=326&amp;focus=cheng-han" aria-label="Cheng Han on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7376 1059 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cheng Han lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7696 1315L7855 1179"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7855 1179h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7696 1315h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7696 1315h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chengdu. <a href="/atlas?year=326&amp;focus=cheng-han">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

